tel 1.0

base breviloquence
import soundness

info   CBOR (RFC 8949) as a typed tree, derived and generated codecs, a streaming parser and HTTP bodies
detail
    _Breviloquence_ reads and writes [CBOR](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8949), the
    binary cousin of JSON, with the same interface Soundness gives its textual siblings: bytes
    parse into a `Cbor` value, case classes convert to and from it with codecs derived from
    their definitions, and navigation, updates and lenses work as they do for JSON and YAML.
    Where no human reads the wire — a device protocol, a cache entry, a message queue — CBOR
    keeps JSON's data model (maps, arrays, integers, floats, text, byte strings, booleans,
    `null` and `undefined`) in a compact binary form, and adds **tags** which annotate a value
    with its meaning.

    A `Cbor` is an immutable tree over a `Cbor.Ast`, the opaque storage type whose accessors
    (`isMap`, `entries`, `key`, `value`, `long`, `string`, …) classify and read one node. A
    value encodes with `in[Cbor]` and a tree decodes with `as[T]`; bytes come from
    `Cbor.Ast.encodable` and go back through `Cbor.Ast.parse`, or, in one step each way,
    `in[Data over Cbor]` and `read[T in Cbor]`:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    val encoded: Cbor = Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Cbor]
    encoded.as[Person]                                     // Person(t"Ada", 36)

    val bytes: Data = Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Data over Cbor]
    bytes.read[Person in Cbor]                             // Person(t"Ada", 36)
    Cbor.unseal(encoded).show                              // t"{\"name\": \"Ada\", \"age\": 36}"
    ```

    Reading is streaming: `read[Cbor]`, `read[Cbor.Ast]` and `read[T in Cbor]` over a
    `Chain[Data]` or a `Stream[Data]` drive the `CborParser`, a stateful capability over a
    `zephyrine.Cursor`, which reads each chunk as it is pulled and never assembles the input
    first. A value split across chunks reads exactly as one which arrived whole, and every
    `Cbor.Error` reports an absolute byte offset.

    Three paths lead from bytes to a value. The **derived** path parses the whole tree and
    decodes it through `Decodable in Cbor`, derived for any case class or sealed hierarchy.
    The **direct** path, `Cbor.Parsable` and `Cbor.Printable`, consumes items straight off
    the parser's rim through a `Cbor.Reader`, or writes them straight into a byte producer,
    with no tree built; `Inlinable.parsable[T]` and `Inlinable.printable[T]` generate such
    instances at compile time, `derives Cbor.Direct` generates both into a companion, and
    `import codecs.direct` selects the generated codec for every record in scope. A third
    option, `import codecs.strict`, makes a record with no direct codec a compile error. With
    no mode imported, a type takes the direct path exactly when it has a `Cbor.Parsable` or
    `Cbor.Printable` of its own, and the derived path otherwise.

    The library has three components:

    - `breviloquence.core` — `Cbor`, `Cbor.Ast`, `CborParser`, `Cbor.Reader`, the derived
      and direct codecs, optics, `Inlinable` and the `optionalityOptions`, `dynamicAccess`
      and `conversions` packages. Compiles for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native.
    - `breviloquence.staged` — an in-macro staging compiler (`InlinableStager`) which lets
      `Inlinable.parsable` evaluate a hand-written `Inlinable` instance, and a sealed sum's
      `Discriminable in Cbor`, while the code compiles. It brings the Scala compiler onto the
      macro classpath, so it is a separate, opt-in component; without it a field whose type
      has only a hand-written instance takes a runtime seam, and a sum is not generated. JVM
      only.
    - `breviloquence.http` — the `postables`, `servables` and `construables` givens which make
      a `Cbor` an `application/cbor` request or response body with telekinesis. JVM only.

    `Cbor` and `Cbor2` are exported to the `soundness` umbrella, with the `parse` extension,
    and each choice package merges with the umbrella's own: a user writes `import soundness.*`
    and then imports a policy by name, since `import soundness.*` does not import givens
    (`import optionalityOptions.strictCborNulls`, `import dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor`,
    `import codecs.direct`).

refer breviloquence.Cbor
refer breviloquence.Cbor.Ast
refer breviloquence.CborParser
refer breviloquence.Inlinable
refer breviloquence.Cbor.Error

entry .Cbor   a CBOR document: an immutable tree, with its codecs, parser hooks and optics
  detail
      A `Cbor` wraps one `Cbor.Ast` node — the root of a document — and is the type every
      encoding produces and every decoding consumes. It is `Dynamic`, so with
      `dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor` in scope a map's fields read as members, an array indexes,
      and an assignment makes an updated copy; it compares structurally with `==`, map
      entries position by position (canonical CBOR orders keys deterministically, so a
      round-tripped document compares equal, but two maps with the same entries in a
      different order do not).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      val person: Cbor = Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Cbor]
      person.name.as[Text]                  // t"Ada"
      (person.age = 40).as[Person]          // Person(t"Ada", 40)
      Cbor.make(name = t"Anna".in[Cbor], age = 30.in[Cbor]).as[Person]
      ```

      The companion holds everything a `Cbor` can do: the `Encodable in Cbor` and
      `Decodable in Cbor` givens for primitives, collections, `Optional`, `Option` and `Map`
      (`Cbor.decodable` and `Cbor.encodable` derive them for any case class or sealed
      hierarchy), the read paths `.aggregable`, `Cbor.aggregableIn` and
      `Cbor.aggregableParsed` which turn chunked bytes into a tree or a value, the write paths
      `Cbor.encodableOver` and `Cbor.encodableOverPrinted` behind `in[Data over Cbor]`, the
      `.lens` and array optics, `.emit` for serializing a block at a time, and the
      direct-codec surface `.Parsable`, `.Printable`, `.Reader` and `.Direct`. All of these
      are in implicit scope: only the policies in `optionalityOptions`, `dynamicAccess`,
      `conversions` and `codecs` are imported by name. Those written here as `Cbor.…` are
      inherited rather than declared: the lower-priority traits `Cbor1` and `Cbor2` declare
      them, and document them.

      Decoding needs a `Tactic[Cbor.Error]` in scope (`strategies.throwUnsafely`, a `safely`
      or `unsafely` block, or a `raises Cbor.Error` clause). A failed read raises
      `Cbor.Error` with a `Cbor.Error.Reason`: malformed input names a byte offset, and a
      failed conversion says which type was found and which was expected, or that a field
      was absent.
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Ast
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Error
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Parsable
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Printable
  refer breviloquence.Inlinable
  refer breviloquence.dynamicAccess
  entry #apply   a map's field by name, or an array's element by index
      `cbor(t"name")` is the value under the key `name`, as a `Cbor`, or a `Cbor` whose root
      is `undefined` if the map has no such key (so a chain of lookups into missing fields
      yields `undefined` rather than failing); looking a field up on a non-map raises
      `Cbor.Error` with `NotType(found, Map)`. `cbor(2)` is the third element of an array,
      and raises `NotType` on anything else.
  entry #selectDynamic   `cbor.field`: a map entry by name, under `dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor`
      The `Dynamic` form of `#apply` with a text key, available only where a
      `(? >: Cbor) is Dynamical` is in scope — `import dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor` for a whole
      scope, or a `dynamically[Cbor]:` block for one expression — so that a misspelt field
      name cannot be mistaken for a method where dynamic access was not intended. Raises
      `Cbor.Error` if the receiver is not a map.
  entry #applyDynamic   `cbor.field(index)`: an element of an array-valued field
      Under `dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor`, `cbor.values(1)` is the second element of the array
      under the key `values`. Raises `Cbor.Error` if the receiver is not a map or the field is
      not an array.
  entry #updateDynamic   `cbor.field = value`: a copy with the entry replaced, added or removed
      Under `dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor`, `person.age = 40` is a new `Cbor` whose `age` entry
      holds the encoding of `40` (any `Encodable in Cbor` value; `conversions.encodableToCbor`
      lets a lens assignment take one too), appended as a new entry if the key was absent.
      `person.age = Unset` removes the entry. Both raise `Cbor.Error` with `NotType(found,
      Map)` if the receiver is not a map.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      case class Named(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

      val person: Cbor = Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Cbor]
      (person.age = 40).as[Person]       // Person(t"Ada", 40)
      (person.age = Unset).as[Named]     // Named(t"Ada", Unset)
      ```
  entry #as   decodes the document as a type, through its `Decodable in Cbor`
      `cbor.as[Person]` runs the type's decoder over the tree and needs a
      `Tactic[Cbor.Error]`. A case class decodes from a map by field name (renamed by a
      `@name[Cbor]` annotation), a sealed hierarchy through its `Discriminable in Cbor`, a
      collection from an array, and a primitive from the matching leaf; an `Optional` or
      `Option` field reads an absent key, a `null` or a mistyped value as
      `optionalityOptions` says. Under a `Validate` scope, every mistyped or absent field
      accrues its own error with a `Pointer` to it, and the case class is constructed only
      when every field was clean.
  entry .ast   wraps an AST node as a `Cbor` document
      `Cbor.ast(node)` is the inverse of `.unseal`: a `Cbor.Ast` — the result of
      `Cbor.Ast.parse`, or a node reached through the accessors — as a document which can be
      decoded, navigated and updated.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(hex"83010203")).as[List[Int]]   // List(1, 2, 3)
      ```
  entry .unseal   the AST node at the root of a document
      `Cbor.unseal(cbor)` exposes the tree as a `Cbor.Ast`, for the accessors (`isTag`,
      `entries`, `key`, …), for `show` in diagnostic notation, and for
      `Cbor.Ast.encodable.encoded`, which gives the document's bytes.
  entry .applyDynamicNamed   `Cbor.make(key = value, …)`: a map built from named arguments
      The companion is `Dynamic` with the single method name `make`, so
      `Cbor.make(name = t"Anna".in[Cbor], age = 30.in[Cbor])` is a map with those entries in
      that order, each argument already a `Cbor`. This is how a map is assembled where no case
      class fits.
  entry .emit   a document's bytes a block at a time, pulled as an iterator or pushed to a function
      Two forms over the same depth-first walk, each needing a `Buffering` in scope for the
      block size. `Cbor.emit(cbor)` is an `Iterator[Data]^` which serializes the next block
      only when it is pulled, without a fiber: a block ends at the first node boundary past
      the buffering capacity, so one long string may overrun it. `Cbor.emit(cbor, deliver)`
      serializes on the caller's thread and hands each block to `deliver` as it fills, the
      shape for writing to a file, a socket or an `OutputStream`. The bytes are exactly those
      `Cbor.Ast.encodable` writes, and `.streamable` wraps the pull form as a `Stream`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Wrapper(values: List[Int], label: Text)

      given Buffering:
        def capacity(substrate: Substrate): Int = 8
        def depth: Int = 2

      val cbor: Cbor = Wrapper(List(1, 2, 300, 70000), t"hello, world").in[Cbor]
      Cbor.emit(cbor).to[List].length                  // several blocks
      Cbor.emit(cbor, block => ())
      ```
  entry .streamable   a `Cbor` as a `Stream[Data]` metered by `Credit`, through `.emit`
      Needs a `Buffering`. This is what lets a document be written with `stream.transfer`, or
      sent wherever a `Streamable by Data` is accepted, without its whole encoding in memory.
  entry .aggregable   reads chunked bytes into a `Cbor`: the given behind `read[Cbor]`
      `Chain(bytes).read[Cbor]` and `stream.read[Cbor]` parse one data item, reading each
      chunk as it is pulled, and reject trailing bytes with `Cbor.Error(Trailing(offset))`.
      Needs a `Tactic[Cbor.Error]`; the whole tree is built, so the input's size bounds the
      memory held.
  entry .aggregableDerived   under `codecs.derived`, the tree path even where a `Cbor.Parsable` is in scope
      `import codecs.derived` selects the derived codecs for every type: this given outranks
      `Cbor.aggregableParsed` and applies exactly where it would, so `read[T in Cbor]` parses the
      tree and decodes it through the type's `Decodable in Cbor` even when a direct parser is
      in scope.
  entry .readableDerived   under `codecs.derived`, `data.read[T in Cbor]` through the tree
      The whole-`Data` counterpart of `.aggregableDerived`, outranking `Cbor.readableParsed`.
  entry .encodableOverDerived   under `codecs.derived`, `in[Data over Cbor]` through the tree
      Outranks `Cbor.encodableOverPrinted` wherever a `Cbor.Printable` is in scope, so the value is
      encoded to a tree through its `Encodable in Cbor` and serialized.
  entry .aggregableStrict   under `codecs.strict`, the direct read path for a type with a `Cbor.Parsable`
      `import codecs.strict` is `codecs.direct` with no fallback: a type with a direct parser
      reads through it, and a type without one meets `Cbor.aggregableStrictFailure`, a compile
      error naming the type, instead of the tree path.
  entry .encodableOverStrict   under `codecs.strict`, `in[Data over Cbor]` through a `Cbor.Printable`
      The writing half of the strict pair; a type with no `Cbor.Printable` meets
      `Cbor.encodableOverStrictFailure`.
  entry .option   decodes an `Option[T]` field, by the same three `optionalityOptions` policies as `Cbor.optional`
      Absent, `null` and faulty values yield `None` under the lenient policies and raise
      `Cbor.Error` under the strict ones; a present value decodes to `Some`.
  entry .optionEncodable   encodes an `Option[T]`: the value's encoding, or `undefined` for `None`
  entry .unit   decodes CBOR `null` as `()`
      Anything but `null` raises `Cbor.Error`: `Absent` for `undefined`, and `NotType(found,
      Null)` otherwise.
  entry .boolean   decodes a CBOR boolean as `Boolean`
      Any other node raises `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, Boolean))`.
  entry .double   decodes a CBOR float as `Double`, or an integer widened to one
      Half, single and double precision floats all parse to a `Double`; an integer node is
      converted. Any other node raises `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, Float))`.
  entry .float   decodes a CBOR float or integer as `Float`
  entry .long   decodes a CBOR integer as `Long`, or a float truncated to one
      An integer outside the range of a `Long` fails at parse time with `Overflow(offset)`;
      a float node is truncated. Any other node raises `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, Integer))`.
  entry .int   decodes a CBOR integer as `Int`
      Through `.long`, then narrowed with `toInt`: a value outside the `Int` range wraps
      rather than failing.
  entry .text   decodes a CBOR text string as `Text`
      Any other node raises `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, TextString))`.
  entry .string   decodes a CBOR text string as `String`
  entry .byteString   decodes a CBOR byte string as frozen `Array[Byte]`, that is, `Data`
      Any other node raises `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, ByteString))`.
  entry .cbor   decodes a `Cbor` as itself, so a field may keep its subtree as a document
  entry .integralEncodable   encodes any `Integral` number as a CBOR integer, through `toLong`
  entry .textEncodable   encodes `Text` as a CBOR text string
  entry .stringEncodable   encodes `String` as a CBOR text string
  entry .doubleEncodable   encodes `Double` as a 64-bit CBOR float
      Floats are always written at 64 bits; the shorter encodings are parsed but never
      produced.
  entry .floatEncodable   encodes `Float` as a 64-bit CBOR float
  entry .intEncodable   encodes `Int` as a CBOR integer, in the shortest head encoding
  entry .longEncodable   encodes `Long` as a CBOR integer, in the shortest head encoding
      A negative value is written as major type 1; the head takes 1, 2, 3, 5 or 9 bytes
      according to the magnitude.
  entry .booleanEncodable   encodes `Boolean` as CBOR `true` or `false`
  entry .unitEncodable   encodes `()` as CBOR `null`
  entry .bytesEncodable   encodes a frozen `Array[Byte]` (`Data`) as a CBOR byte string
  entry .cborEncodable   encodes a `Cbor` as itself
  entry .listEncodable   encodes a `List[T]` as a CBOR array, through the element's `Encodable in Cbor`
      Captures the element codec, which is taken by name so that a recursive type (a `Tree`
      with `children: List[Tree]`) derives.
  entry .setEncodable   encodes a `Set[T]` as a CBOR array of its elements, in the set's order
  entry .seriesEncodable   encodes a `Sequence[T]` as a CBOR array
  entry .collectionDecodable   decodes a CBOR array into any `Iterable` collection with a `Factory`
      The general instance for stdlib collection types; `.listDecodable`, `.setDecodable`
      and `.seriesDecodable` are its counterparts for Soundness's own `List`, `Set` and
      `Sequence`, which are opaque and so do not match `Iterable`. A non-array node raises
      `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, Array))`. Captures the tactic and the element decoder.
  entry .listDecodable   decodes a CBOR array as a `List[T]`
      The element decoder is taken by name, so a type recursive through a `List` (a `Tree`
      with `children: List[Tree]`) derives without a hand-written instance.
  entry .setDecodable   decodes a CBOR array as a `Set[T]`
  entry .seriesDecodable   decodes a CBOR array as a `Sequence[T]`
  entry .mapDecodable   decodes a CBOR map with text keys as a `Map[K, V]`
      Each key decodes through `K`'s `Decodable in Text` and each value through `V`'s
      `Decodable in Cbor`; a key which is not a text string raises
      `Cbor.Error(NonStringKey)`. A non-map node decodes as an empty map.
  entry .mapEncodable   encodes a `Map[K, V]` as a CBOR map with text keys
      Keys are written through `K`'s `Encodable in Text`, in the map's iteration order.
  entry .lens   `cbor.lens(_.field = value)`: a `Lens` from a document onto one of its map entries
      Each name becomes a `name is Lens from Cbor onto Cbor` which reads the entry with
      `#selectDynamic` and writes it with an immutable update, so `team.lens(_.lead.name =
      t"Bob")` rebuilds the path from the root. It needs a `(? >: Cbor) is Dynamical`
      (`dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor`) and a `Tactic[Cbor.Error]`; with
      `conversions.encodableToCbor` imported, the assigned value may be any `Encodable in
      Cbor` rather than a `Cbor`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor
      import conversions.encodableToCbor

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      case class Team(lead: Person, size: Int)

      val team: Cbor = Team(Person(t"John", 40), 3).in[Cbor]
      team.lens(_.size = 5).as[Team]               // Team(Person(t"John", 40), 5)
      team.lens(_.lead.name = t"Bob").as[Team]     // Team(Person(t"Bob", 40), 3)
      summon["size" is Lens from Cbor onto Cbor](team).as[Int]   // 3
      ```
  entry .ordinalOptical   `cbor.lens(_.values(Sec) = x)`: an optic onto one element of an array by ordinal
      An `Ordinal` (`Prim`, `Sec`, `Ter`, …) applied inside a lens path selects one element
      of an array-valued entry; an ordinal past the end, or a non-array, leaves the document
      unchanged.
  entry .eachOptical   `cbor.lens(_.values(Each) = x)`: an optic over every element of an array
      Applies the update to every element; a non-array is left unchanged.
  entry .filterOptical   `cbor.lens(_.values(Filter[Cbor](p)) = x)`: an optic over the elements a predicate selects
      Applies the update to each element for which the predicate holds and keeps the rest.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor
      import conversions.encodableToCbor

      case class Wrapper(values: List[Int], label: Text)
      val list: Cbor = Wrapper(List(1, 2, 3), t"hi").in[Cbor]

      list.lens(_.values(Sec) = 9).as[Wrapper]                          // Wrapper(List(1, 9, 3), t"hi")
      list.lens(_.values(Each) = 0).as[Wrapper]                         // Wrapper(List(0, 0, 0), t"hi")
      list.lens(_.values(Filter[Cbor](_.as[Int] > 1)) = 0).as[Wrapper]  // Wrapper(List(1, 0, 0), t"hi")
      ```
  entry .abstractable   a `Cbor` as an HTTP body: `application/cbor` and the encoded bytes
      The `Abstractable across HttpStreams` given from which telekinesis derives a request or
      response body: `cbor.generic` is the pair of the media type and the bytes. The
      `breviloquence.http` component's `postables.cborPostable` and `servables.cborServable`
      are the named forms for a client's `post` and a server's response, and a body parses
      back through `.aggregable`.
  entry .discriminatedUnion   a `Discriminable in Cbor` for a sealed hierarchy, by a named map key
      `given (Status is Discriminable in Cbor) = Cbor.discriminatedUnion(t"kind")` declares,
      once, that a `Status` encodes as its variant's map with an extra `kind` entry holding
      the variant's name (renamed by `@name[Cbor]` on the case), and decodes by reading the
      same entry back. The result is a `.DiscriminantKey`, the one shape the direct-codec
      generator can recognise, so a sum with this instance can also be generated by
      `Inlinable.parsable` when `breviloquence.staged` is on the compile classpath.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      enum Status:
        case Active(since: Int)
        case Retired

      given (Status is Discriminable in Cbor) = Cbor.discriminatedUnion(t"kind")

      (Status.Active(2020): Status).in[Cbor].as[Status]   // Status.Active(2020)
      ```
  entry .DiscriminantKey   the map-key-discriminated `Discriminable in Cbor`, as a class the generator can recognise
    detail
        The class `Cbor.discriminatedUnion` returns: a `Discriminable` with `Form = Cbor`
        which writes the variant name under `#key`, reads it back from the same entry, and
        strips the entry before a variant is decoded as its product. It is a named class, not
        an anonymous instance, because a generated direct parser dispatches on the
        discriminant monomorphically and must extract the key at expansion time.
    entry #key   the map key which holds a variant's name
    entry #rewrite   adds the discriminator entry to a variant's encoded map
    entry #discriminate   the variant name a document carries under the key, or `Unset`
        `Unset` when the document is not a map, has no such key, or the key's value is not
        text; the derived decoder then raises `Cbor.Error(Absent)`.
    entry #variant   the document with the discriminator entry removed, ready to decode as a product
  entry .Ast   one CBOR node: the opaque storage type behind a document, with its accessors and codecs
    detail
        `Cbor.Ast` is the representation of a CBOR data item: an opaque union over the JVM
        values which store each major type — a `Long` for an integer, a `Double` for a float,
        a `String` for a text string, a frozen `Array[Byte]` for a byte string, a `Boolean`,
        `.CborNull` for `null`, `Unset` for `undefined`, a `.Tag` for a tagged value, and a
        frozen `Array[Any]` for an array or a map (an array's backing array has odd length,
        padded with a sentinel, and a map's has even length, keys and values alternating,
        which is how the two are told apart). A node is never pattern-matched: the accessors
        in the companion classify it (`unset`, `nullary`, `isInteger`, `isFloat`,
        `isTextString`, `isByteString`, `isBoolean`, `isTag`, `isArray`, `isMap`,
        `primitive`) and read it (`long`, `double`, `string`, `byteString`, `boolean`, `tag`,
        `array`, `elements`, `element`, `entries`, `key`, `value`, `index`), and each is in
        implicit scope wherever a `Cbor.Ast` is used, through `import soundness.*` alone.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val ast: Cbor.Ast = Cbor.Ast.parse(hex"a2 6161 01 6162 82 02 03")   // {"a": 1, "b": [2, 3]}
        ast.isMap && ast.entries == 2                 // true
        ast.key(0).string                             // "a"
        ast.value(1).element(1).long                  // 3L
        ast.show                                      // t"{\"a\": 1, \"b\": [2, 3]}"
        Cbor.Ast.encodable.encoded(ast).length        // 9
        ```

        A node is parsed from bytes with `parse` (an extension on the companion, from
        `breviloquence.parse`), read from chunked bytes with `read[Cbor.Ast]` through
        `.aggregable`, serialized through `.encodable`, and rendered in RFC 8949 diagnostic
        notation through `.showable`. `Cbor.ast` wraps a node as a document and
        `Cbor.unseal` takes the root node back out.
    refer breviloquence.Cbor
    refer breviloquence.parse
    refer breviloquence.CborParser
    entry .apply   wraps a storage value — a `Long`, a `String`, a `Tag`, `Unset`, … — as a node
        `Cbor.Ast(42L)`, `Cbor.Ast("text")`, `Cbor.Ast(Unset)` for `undefined`,
        `Cbor.Ast(Cbor.CborNull)` for `null`, or `Cbor.Ast(Cbor.Tag(1L, 1363896240L))`. The
        argument must be one of the `.CborTypes` members; an array or map is built with
        `.array` or `.map` instead, which lay the backing array out correctly.
    entry .map   a map node from parallel arrays of keys and values
        `Cbor.Ast.map(keys, values)` interleaves the two frozen `Array[Any]`s of nodes into
        the even-length backing array a map node is; the arrays must be the same length.
    entry .array   a node's elements as an array, or an array node from an array of elements
        As a companion method, `Cbor.Ast.array(elements)` makes an array node from a frozen
        `Array[Any]` of nodes, padding it with a sentinel if its length is even so that it
        can be told from a map. As an accessor, `ast.array` is the elements of an array node
        as a frozen `Array[Cbor.Ast]` (without the padding), and raises
        `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, Array))` on any other node.
    entry .length   the number of elements an array node holds, discounting the padding
        The accessor `elements` is the same count; `.size` is the entry count of a map node.
    entry .size   the number of entries a map node holds
        The accessor `entries` is the same count.
    entry .Sentinel   the padding object at the end of an even-count array's backing array
        An implementation detail of the storage layout, public because the accessors are
        inline: it is never a node's value, and `elements` never counts it.
    entry .encodable   serializes a node to its CBOR bytes: `Cbor.Ast.encodable.encoded(ast)`
        The `Encodable in Data` given, and so `ast.encode`: the tree is written depth first,
        with integers in the shortest head encoding, floats always at 64 bits, text as UTF-8,
        and arrays and maps length-prefixed (never indefinite-length, which is parsed but not
        produced). The bytes are exactly those a generated `Cbor.Printable` writes and
        `Cbor.emit` yields block by block.
    entry .aggregable   reads chunked bytes into a node: the given behind `read[Cbor.Ast]`
        `Chain(bytes).read[Cbor.Ast]` and `stream.read[Cbor.Ast]` parse one data item through
        the `CborParser`, pulling each chunk as the parser needs it, and reject trailing bytes
        with `Cbor.Error(Trailing(offset))`. Needs a `Tactic[Cbor.Error]`.
    entry .showable   renders a node in RFC 8949 diagnostic notation: `ast.show`
        The human-readable rendering the specification defines, the format's counterpart of
        a hex dump which can be read: integers and floats as numbers (`NaN`, `Infinity`,
        `-Infinity` spelled out), text strings quoted with `\"`, `\\`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t` and
        `\uXXXX` escapes, byte strings as `h'0102'`, booleans, `null` and `undefined` by
        name, a tag as `1(1363896240)`, arrays as `[1, 2, 3]` and maps as `{"a": 1}`. It is
        what to reach for when a message is not what it should be.

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label A record, encoded
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
              val bytes: Data = Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Data over Cbor]
              bytes.length   // 15
        frame
          label The bytes, in diagnostic notation
          code
              Cbor.Ast.parse(bytes).show   // t"{\"name\": \"Ada\", \"age\": 36}"
        frame
          label And decoded back
          code
              bytes.read[Person in Cbor]   // Person(t"Ada", 36)
        ##
        ```
    entry .unset   whether a node is `undefined` (or absent): it is `Unset`
    entry .nullary   whether a node is CBOR `null`
        `null` and `undefined` are distinct on the wire (0xF6 and 0xF7) and distinct here:
        `null` is the `.CborNull` sentinel, so that it cannot collide with the null-backed
        `Unset` which represents `undefined` and an absent key.
    entry .isInteger   whether a node is an integer (major types 0 and 1), stored as a `Long`
    entry .isFloat   whether a node is a float, stored as a `Double` whatever its wire width
    entry .isTextString   whether a node is a text string (major type 3), stored as a `String`
    entry .isByteString   whether a node is a byte string (major type 2), stored as a frozen `Array[Byte]`
    entry .isBoolean   whether a node is `true` or `false`
    entry .isTag   whether a node is a tagged value, a `Cbor.Tag`
    entry .isArray   whether a node is an array (major type 4)
    entry .isMap   whether a node is a map (major type 5)
    entry .primitive   which kind of node this is, as a `Cbor.Error.Primitive`
        The classification an error reports: `Integer`, `Float`, `TextString`, `ByteString`,
        `Boolean`, `Map`, `Array`, `Tag`, `Undefined` or `Null`.
    entry .elements   the number of elements of an array node
        Zero-cost on an array; on any other node the result is meaningless, so check
        `isArray` first.
    entry .element   an array node's element by index, unchecked
        `ast.element(i)` reads the backing array directly: the index must be below
        `elements`, and the node must be an array.
    entry .entries   the number of entries of a map node
    entry .key   a map node's key by entry index, unchecked
        `ast.key(i)` for `i` below `entries`. A key is any node, though the derived codecs
        only use text keys and ignore an entry under any other.
    entry .value   a map node's value by entry index, unchecked
    entry .index   the entry index of a text key in a map node, or `-1` if absent
        A linear scan comparing each key to the `String`; the first match wins, which is
        why a repeated key reads as its first occurrence.
    entry .long   an integer node's value, or a float's truncated; raises `NotType` otherwise
        Any other node raises `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, Integer))`, or `Absent` for
        `undefined`. Under an accruing `Validate` scope the error is registered and `0L` is
        returned so that sibling fields keep accruing; the derived decoder never constructs
        from it.
    entry .double   a float node's value, or an integer's widened; raises `NotType` otherwise
    entry .string   a text string node's value as a `String`; raises `NotType` otherwise
    entry .byteString   a byte string node's bytes as a frozen `Array[Byte]`; raises `NotType` otherwise
    entry .boolean   a boolean node's value; raises `NotType` otherwise
    entry .tag   a tagged node as a `Cbor.Tag`; raises `NotType` otherwise
        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val ast: Cbor.Ast = Cbor.Ast.parse(hex"c11a514b67b0")   // tag 1 around 1363896240
        ast.isTag && ast.tag.tag == 1L                          // true
        ast.tag.value == 1363896240L                            // true
        ```
  entry .Tag   a tagged value: the tag number and the node it annotates
    detail
        CBOR's tags (major type 6) annotate a value with its meaning — tag 1 is an epoch
        time, tag 2 a big integer, and the registry runs to hundreds. A parsed tag keeps both
        its number and its content, so a document carrying tags round-trips without losing
        them; nothing in the library interprets a tag's meaning. Two tags are equal when
        their numbers and values are. The derived codecs do not produce tags, and decoding a
        tagged node as a primitive raises `Cbor.Error(NotType(Tag, …))`; read the tag with
        `ast.tag` and decode its `#value`.
    entry #tag   the tag number
    entry #value   the tagged node, as `Any`; cast it to `Cbor.Ast` to read it
  entry .CborNull   the sentinel which stores a CBOR `null`, distinct from `Unset`
      An object and the type of that object. `undefined` (0xF7) and an absent key are the
      null-backed `Unset`; `null` (0xF6) would be the same JVM `null`, so it is this
      sentinel instead, which `nullary` tests for.
  entry .CborInteger   the storage type of an integer node: `Long`
  entry .CborFloat   the storage type of a float node: `Double`
  entry .CborText   the storage type of a text string node: `String`
  entry .CborBytes   the storage type of a byte string node: a frozen `Array[Byte]`
  entry .CborArray   the storage type of an array node: a frozen `Array[Any]` of odd length
  entry .CborMap   the storage type of a map node: a frozen `Array[Any]` of even length, keys and values alternating
  entry .CborBoolean   the storage type of a boolean node: `Boolean`
  entry .CborUndefined   the storage type of `undefined`: `Unset`
  entry .CborTypes   the union of every storage type a `Cbor.Ast` may be
      `CborInteger | CborFloat | CborText | CborBytes | CborArray | CborMap | CborBoolean |
      CborNull | CborUndefined | Tag`: what `Cbor.Ast.apply` accepts.
  entry .Error   raised when CBOR cannot be parsed or a node cannot be decoded as the type asked for
    detail
        Error 595: _could not process the CBOR value because `reason`_, with the
        `.Reason`'s own number as the error's second component. Raised by `Cbor.Ast.parse`
        and every `read[…]` for malformed input, with the absolute byte offset at which the
        fault was met — input which stops mid-value names the offset of the byte which
        should have followed, trailing bytes name where the surplus begins, and a reserved
        head byte names its offset and its value — and by every decoder for a node of the
        wrong kind, a missing field or a non-text map key. It needs a `Diagnostics` in scope
        to construct, as every `fulminate.Error` does.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        capture[Cbor.Error](Cbor.Ast.parse(hex"830102")).reason match
          case Cbor.Error.Reason.Truncated(offset) => offset   // 3: three elements promised, two given
          case Cbor.Error.Reason.Trailing(offset)  => offset
          case _                                   => -1L
        ```

        Under a `Validate` scope, decoding accrues one error per faulty field, each with a
        `Pointer` naming the field (`lead.name` for a nested record).
    entry #reason   why the value could not be processed, as a `Cbor.Error.Reason`
    entry .Reason   the specific fault: where the input broke, or what mismatched
      detail
          A `Clarification` enum, each case numbered. Cases 1 to 7 are parse faults, and carry
          an absolute byte offset into the input, counted from its start whatever the chunking;
          cases 8 to 11 are decoding faults, about the tree:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `Truncated(offset)`  1  _the input was truncated at byte `offset`_  the item needed a byte which was not there
          row  `Reserved(offset, byte)`  2  _a reserved CBOR head byte `byte` was found at byte `offset`_  additional information 28 to 30
          row  `BadSimpleValue(offset, value)`  3  _an invalid simple value `value` was found at byte `offset`_  a two-byte simple value below 32
          row  `InvalidUtf8(offset)`  4  _invalid UTF-8 was found at byte `offset`_  in a text string
          row  `Overflow(offset)`  5  _an integer too large for Long was found at byte `offset`_  a 64-bit unsigned integer, or a negative one, beyond `Long`
          row  `UnexpectedBreak(offset)`  6  _an unexpected break stop code was found at byte `offset`_  0xFF outside an indefinite-length item
          row  `Trailing(offset)`  7  _unexpected trailing bytes were found from byte `offset`_  the item ended but the input did not
          row  `OutOfRange`  8  _the array index was out of range_
          row  `NotType(found, expected)`  9  _the CBOR value had type `found` instead of `expected`_  each a `.Primitive`
          row  `NonStringKey`  10  _the map key was not a string_  decoding a `Map`
          row  `Absent`  11  _the CBOR value was not present_  a required field's key or a sealed hierarchy's discriminator was missing, or an `Optional` was absent under `optionalityOptions.strictCborAbsence`
          ```
      entry #number   the reason's number, 1 to 11, which the error reports as its second component
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry .Primitive   the kinds of CBOR node an error names: what was found and what was expected
      detail
          `Integer`, `Float`, `ByteString`, `TextString`, `Array`, `Map`, `Tag`, `Boolean`,
          `Null` and `Undefined`: the classification `Cbor.Ast`'s `primitive` accessor
          gives, used by `Reason.NotType`.
      entry .communicable   renders each kind as a message: `integer`, `byte string`, `text string`, …
  entry .Reader   the restricted rim of the parser a `Cbor.Parsable` reads through: one item per call
    detail
        A `Cbor.Reader` wraps a `CborParser` and the `Tactic[Cbor.Error]` of the read which
        made it, and is handed to a `Cbor.Parsable`'s `parse` so that the instance can
        consume data items straight off the input, with no `Cbor.Ast` built. Each method
        consumes exactly one item, or one structural step; failures abort through the
        carried tactic, so a `parse` body needs no error vocabulary of its own. Values and
        failures agree with the `Cbor.Ast` accessors exactly — an integer coerces to a
        float and a float to an integer, as `long` and `double` do on a node — so a direct
        read and a tree read yield equal values.

        It is an exclusive, stateful capability (`caps.ExclusiveCapability, caps.Stateful`),
        owned by one `parse` call for its duration: every reading method is an `update def`,
        callable only through a `Cbor.Reader^`, and nothing of it may be retained after the
        call. Only the library's read paths construct one; a hand-written instance receives
        it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Point(x: Int, y: Int)

        given (Point is Cbor.Parsable) = Cbor.Parsable: reader =>
          var x = 0
          var y = 0
          val count = reader.openMap()
          var remaining = count

          while remaining != 0 && !(count < 0 && reader.breakEnd()) do
            reader.keyName() match
              case "x"  => x = reader.int()
              case "y"  => y = reader.int()
              case _    => reader.skipValue()
            remaining -= 1

          Point(x, y)

        hex"a2 6178 03 6179 04".read[Point in Cbor]   // Point(3, 4)
        ```
    refer breviloquence.Cbor.Parsable
    refer breviloquence.CborParser
    entry .KeyOpaque   the value `#keyWord` returns for a key it cannot pack: `-2L`
        Impossible as a packed key, whose bytes are all 7-bit ASCII. A caller which sees it
        takes the `#keyName` step instead.
    entry .of   reasserts a reader which travelled as an `AnyRef` carrier, for generated parsers
        `Cbor.Reader.of(carrier)` casts a neutral reference back to a `Cbor.Reader^`. It is
        the entry point of the parsers `Inlinable.parsable` splices into user modules, where
        the generated body receives the reader capture-erased; hand-written code has no use
        for it.
    entry #long   consumes an integer item as a `Long`, or a float truncated to one
    entry #int   consumes an integer item as an `Int`
    entry #double   consumes a float item as a `Double`, or an integer widened to one
    entry #boolean   consumes a `true` or `false` item
    entry #text   consumes a text string item as `Text`
    entry #string   consumes a text string item as a `String`
    entry #byteString   consumes a byte string item as a frozen `Array[Byte]`
    entry #hasUndefined   whether the next item is `undefined`, without consuming it
        The peek an optional wrapper makes to map a wire `undefined` (0xF7) to an absent
        value, exactly as the tree path's `Cbor.optional` does.
    entry #undefined   consumes an `undefined` item
    entry #openMap   opens a map: its entry count, or `-1` for an indefinite-length map
        An indefinite-length map ends with a break stop code, consumed by `#breakEnd`. Any
        other item is consumed whole and reads as an empty map — every field absent — which
        is what the tree path's record decoder does with a non-map.
    entry #openArray   opens an array: its element count, or `-1` for an indefinite-length array
        A non-array item fails with `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, Array))`, as the tree
        accessor `array` does.
    entry #breakEnd   consumes a break stop code if one is next: the end of an indefinite-length item
        `true` when the break was there and consumed, `false` (consuming nothing) when the
        next item is another element or entry.
    entry #keyWord   the next map key packed into a `Long`, for comparison against literals
        Generated parsers compile field names to immediates: a definite-length, one-to-sixteen
        byte, 7-bit-clean text key packs into two words, the low one returned and the high
        one left in `#keyHigh`, and the key is consumed. A key which cannot be packed yields
        `.KeyOpaque` and consumes nothing, so the caller takes `#keyName` instead. A key split
        across a chunk boundary is buffered whole first, so it packs as one which arrived
        whole.
    entry #keyHigh   the high word of the key `#keyWord` last packed
    entry #keyName   consumes the next map key: a text key's content, or `null` for a non-text key
        A non-text key's entry is ignored by the record decoders, so a caller which sees
        `null` skips the entry's value with `#skipValue` and continues.
    entry #value   consumes one whole item as a `Cbor` document
        The seam back to the tree world: a field whose type has only a `Decodable in Cbor` is
        read through this and decoded, materializing just that field's subtree.
    entry #skipValue   consumes one whole item, building nothing
        For an unknown key: a string's content is skipped, not buffered, so skipping is
        bounded-memory whatever the item's size, and a malformed item fails exactly as it
        would when parsed.
    entry #discriminant   the text value under a key of the upcoming map, leaving the reader where it was
        The dispatch primitive for a sealed hierarchy: the discriminant entry may appear
        anywhere in the map, so the map is scanned ahead and the reader rewound to its start
        before the chosen variant parses it. `Unset` when the item is not a map, has no such
        key, or the key's value is not text. The map is held in the buffer for the scan's
        duration, the one place a direct read holds more than a single item.
  entry .Parsable   the direct-parsing typeclass: a type which consumes CBOR items off a `Cbor.Reader`
    detail
        `Cbor.Parsable` is the direct-parsing counterpart of `Decodable in Cbor`: where a
        decoder walks a materialized tree, an instance's `parse(reader)` consumes data items
        straight off the input through a `Cbor.Reader`, so `read[T in Cbor]` instantiates
        the value with no tree built and holds only the item being read. It is the opt-in
        surface for the direct path: an explicit instance (`Cbor.Parsable(reader => …)`),
        one generated at compile time by `Inlinable.parsable[T]`, one generated into the
        companion by `derives Cbor.Direct`, or `import codecs.direct` for every record in
        scope. Without one of these, no read changes behaviour. A field type without an
        instance bridges through `.fromDecodable`, which parses the field's subtree and
        decodes it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
        given (Person is Cbor.Parsable) = Inlinable.parsable[Person]

        val bytes: Data = Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Data over Cbor]
        bytes.read[Person in Cbor]          // parsed without building a tree
        Chain(bytes).read[Person in Cbor]   // and over chunks, as they are pulled
        ```

        An instance is a `distillate.Parsable` with `Transport = Cbor` and `Reader =
        Cbor.Reader`; `#absent` says what a field of the type yields when its key is missing
        from a map. The companion's `generated` given (inherited from `Cbor.Parsable2`) is the
        `codecs.direct` instance, and `.declared` the `derives Cbor.Direct` one.
    refer breviloquence.Cbor.Reader
    refer breviloquence.Inlinable
    refer breviloquence.Cbor.Printable
    refer breviloquence.Cbor.Direct
    entry #Transport   the format this parser reads: `Cbor`
    entry #Reader   the reader it reads through: `Cbor.Reader`
    entry #absent   what a field of this type yields when its key is absent from the map
        Raises `Cbor.Error(Absent)` unless overridden; `.fromDecodable` overrides it to decode
        `undefined` through the bridged decoder, which is how an `Optional` field bridges to
        `Unset`.
    entry .apply   an instance from a function over the reader: `Cbor.Parsable(reader => …)`
        The function receives a `Cbor.Reader^` and must consume exactly one item. The
        instance captures the function.
    entry .fromDecodable   an instance which parses one item as a tree and decodes it with a `Decodable in Cbor`
        The universal bridge from the tree world, and the one-line escape hatch when a custom
        decoder must beat a generated direct parser: `Cbor.Parsable.fromDecodable(decoder)`.
        Its `#absent` decodes `undefined` through the decoder.
    entry .declared   the parser a type generated into its companion with `derives Cbor.Direct`
        A given which resolves through `Cbor.Direct[T]`, so a record declared with the
        `derives` clause is parsed directly with no import.
    entry .missing   raises `Cbor.Error(Absent)` for a required field whose key was not in the map
        Public because generated parsers, spliced into user modules, call it.
    entry .parseField   calls a nominal `Cbor.Parsable` from a generated parser, with the reader as a carrier
        The call point for a hand-written or recursive instance in a field position of
        generated code, which is capture-erased; both arguments travel as `AnyRef` and the
        capability is reasserted here. Not for hand-written code.
    entry .absentField   calls a nominal `Cbor.Parsable`'s `absent` from a generated parser
    entry .Direct   the base class of generated parsers, receiving the reader as a carrier
      detail
          An abstract `Cbor.Parsable` whose `parse` forwards to `#parseCarrier` with the
          reader as an `AnyRef`: generated code is capture-erased, so the capability is
          asserted at this rim rather than in the generated body. `Inlinable.parsable`
          extends it; hand-written code uses `Cbor.Parsable.apply`.
  entry .Printable   the direct-printing typeclass: a type which writes its CBOR bytes straight into a producer
    detail
        `Cbor.Printable` is the writing counterpart of `Cbor.Parsable`, and the direct
        counterpart of `Encodable in Cbor`: `write(value, out)` puts the value's bytes into a
        `Producer.Bytes` with no tree built, and `in[Data over Cbor]` uses an instance
        wherever one exists. The bytes are exactly those the derived encoding writes. Its
        surface is an explicit instance, `Inlinable.printable[T]`, `derives Cbor.Direct` and
        `import codecs.direct`; a field type without an instance bridges through
        `.fromEncodable`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
        given (Person is Cbor.Printable) = Inlinable.printable[Person]

        Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Data over Cbor]   // the same bytes, written directly
        Cbor.Printable.print(Person(t"Ada", 36))
        ```

        `.print` gives the bytes whole and `.emit` hands them on a block at a time, so a
        value can be sent without its whole encoding ever being held. The `write…` methods
        are the leaf writers generated printers call.
    refer breviloquence.Cbor.Parsable
    refer breviloquence.Inlinable
    entry #write   writes a value's CBOR bytes into a byte producer
    entry .print   a value's whole CBOR bytes through its `Cbor.Printable`
        `Cbor.Printable.print(value)` collects what `#write` produces into one `Data`.
    entry .emit   a value's bytes in blocks of the `Buffering` capacity, each handed to a function as it fills
        `Cbor.Printable.emit(value, deliver)` serializes on the caller's thread and never
        builds an array of the whole value: the shape for writing a large record to a file or
        a socket. Needs the type's `Cbor.Printable` and a `Buffering`.
    entry .fromEncodable   an instance which encodes a value to a tree and writes the tree
        The bridge from the tree world, for a field type with only an `Encodable in Cbor`, or
        for forcing a custom encoder over a generated printer.
    entry .declared   the printer a type generated into its companion with `derives Cbor.Direct`
    entry .present   whether a field's encoded tree is written at all: `false` for `undefined`
        The derived encoding leaves out a field whose encoding is `undefined`, such as an
        unset `Optional`; generated printers ask this before writing a bridged field.
    entry .writeHead   writes a major type's head with a count: the leaf writer for an array or map head
        `writeHead(out, 5, entries)` opens a map of that many entries; `4` opens an array.
        The producer travels as an `AnyRef` carrier, as in every `write…` method, because
        generated code is capture-erased; hand-written code uses `Cbor.Ast.encodable` or
        `Cbor.emit`.
    entry .writeLong   writes an integer in the shortest head encoding, negative as major type 1
    entry .writeDouble   writes a 64-bit float
    entry .writeText   writes a text string, as UTF-8 with its length head
    entry .writeBytes   writes a byte string with its length head
    entry .writeBoolean   writes `true` or `false`
    entry .writeAst   writes a whole `Cbor.Ast` node: the seam for a field kept as a document
    entry .writeField   calls a nominal `Cbor.Printable` from a generated printer, with the producer as a carrier
        The call point for a hand-written or recursive instance in a field position. Not
        for hand-written code.
    entry .Direct   the base class of generated printers, receiving the producer as a carrier
      detail
          An abstract `Cbor.Printable` whose `write` forwards to `#writeCarrier` with the
          producer as an `AnyRef`, as `Cbor.Parsable.Direct` does for parsers.
          `Inlinable.printable` extends it.
  entry .Parsable2   the trait which holds the `codecs.direct` parser given
    hidden
    detail
        Mixed into the `Cbor.Parsable` companion so that its one given sits below the
        companion's `declared` instance; not for direct use.
    entry #generated   under `codecs.direct`, a generated `Cbor.Parsable` for any record
        An inline given conditional on `Codecs.Direct` and on `Inlinable.Generates[T]`, the
        evidence that the generator builds the type structurally as a record: a type which
        is not a record — a generic case class, a sealed hierarchy — fails the evidence and
        keeps the derived path unless it has an instance of its own. Expands
        `Inlinable.parsable[T]` where it is needed.
  entry .Printable2   the trait which holds the `codecs.direct` printer given
    hidden
    detail
        Mixed into the `Cbor.Printable` companion; not for direct use.
    entry #generated   under `codecs.direct`, a generated `Cbor.Printable` for any record
        The writing counterpart of `Cbor.Parsable.generated`: expands
        `Inlinable.printable[T]` for any type with `Inlinable.Generates[T]` evidence.
  entry .Direct   `derives Cbor.Direct`: a record's generated parser and printer, made once into its companion
    detail
        A `Self`-typed typeclass has no type parameters, so neither `Cbor.Parsable` nor
        `Cbor.Printable` can appear in a `derives` clause; `Cbor.Direct[T]` is the carrier
        which can. `case class Foo(…) derives Cbor.Direct` generates `Foo`'s parser and
        printer once, into its companion, where the `declared` givens of `Cbor.Parsable`
        and `Cbor.Printable` find them, so `Foo` reads and writes directly with no import
        and no per-use expansion.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Declared(id: Int, name: Text) derives Cbor.Direct

        Declared(5, t"five").in[Data over Cbor].read[Declared in Cbor]   // Declared(5, t"five")
        ```
    refer breviloquence.Cbor.Parsable
    refer breviloquence.Cbor.Printable
    refer breviloquence.Inlinable
    entry .derived   the `derives` hook: `Inlinable.parsable[T]` and `Inlinable.printable[T]`, paired
    entry #parsable   the generated `Cbor.Parsable`
    entry #printable   the generated `Cbor.Printable`

entry .Cbor1   the trait which holds the direct-path givens of `object Cbor`, above the derived path
  hidden
  detail
      Mixed into `object Cbor` between `Cbor2` and the object's own members, so that its
      givens outrank the derived path and are outranked by the `codecs.derived` and
      `codecs.strict` pairs; not for direct use. Its members — `aggregableParsed`,
      `readableParsed`, `encodableOverPrinted`, `aggregableStrictFailure` and
      `encodableOverStrictFailure` — are documented here; `object Cbor` inherits them.
  entry #aggregableParsed   the direct read path: a `Cbor.Parsable` drives the parser, no tree built
      In scope for any type with a `(value is Cbor.Parsable)^`, above `Cbor.aggregableIn`:
      `read[T in Cbor]` over a `Chain[Data]` or a `Stream[Data]` then consumes items straight
      off the `CborParser` through a `Cbor.Reader`, holding at most the one item being read
      (a sum's discriminant scan holds its map), so a large document streams in bounded
      memory. Trailing bytes are rejected exactly as on the tree path. The given captures the
      parsable and the `Tactic[Cbor.Error]` it uses; a summon which names its type must allow
      the capture (`summon[((T in Cbor) is Aggregable by Data)^]`), while resolution through
      `read` needs nothing.
  entry #readableParsed   `data.read[T in Cbor]` for a whole `Data`, parsed in place through a `Cbor.Parsable`
      The in-memory counterpart of `#aggregableParsed`: when the whole content is already a
      `Data`, it is parsed where it lies rather than wrapped in a one-element chain. Captures
      the parsable and the tactic, as `#aggregableParsed` does.
  entry #encodableOverPrinted   `in[Data over Cbor]` written directly through a `Cbor.Printable`
      In scope for any type with a `(value is Cbor.Printable)^`, above `Cbor.encodableOver`: the
      value's bytes are written straight into a byte producer, with no tree built, and are
      exactly those the derived encoding writes.
  entry #aggregableStrictFailure   under `codecs.strict`, the compile error for a type with no direct parser
      An inline given selected for every type the strict pair does not take, which then
      reports _breviloquence: `T` has no direct codec_ at the use site. It exists so that
      `read[T in Cbor]` never silently falls back to the tree under `codecs.strict`.
  entry #encodableOverStrictFailure   under `codecs.strict`, the compile error for a type with no direct printer
      The writing counterpart of `#aggregableStrictFailure`, for `in[Data over Cbor]`.

entry .Cbor2   the trait which holds the derived-path givens of `object Cbor`, at the lowest priority
  hidden
  detail
      Mixed into `object Cbor` beneath everything else, and exported to the `soundness`
      umbrella alongside `Cbor` so that its givens stay reachable there; not for direct use.
      Its members — `decodable`, `encodable`, `optional`, `optionalEncodable`,
      `aggregableIn`, `encodableOver`, `DecodableDerivation` and `EncodableDerivation` — are
      documented here; `object Cbor` inherits them.
  entry #aggregableIn   `read[T in Cbor]` over chunked bytes: parse the tree, then decode it
      `source.read[Person in Cbor]` is shorthand for `source.read[Cbor].as[Person]` for any
      `Decodable in Cbor` type. It is the derived read path; `Cbor.aggregableParsed` outranks it
      whenever the type has a `Cbor.Parsable`, so the same expression reads directly, with no
      tree, as soon as a direct parser is in scope. Needs a `Tactic[Cbor.Error]`.
  entry #encodableOver   `in[Data over Cbor]`: a value's CBOR bytes through its tree
      The write-side mirror of `read[T in Cbor]`: `person.in[Data over Cbor]` encodes the
      value to a `Cbor` and serializes it. `Cbor.encodableOverPrinted` outranks it whenever the
      type has a `Cbor.Printable`, writing the same bytes with no tree.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Data over Cbor].read[Person in Cbor]   // Person(t"Ada", 36)
      ```
  entry #decodable   derives a `Decodable in Cbor` for any case class, sealed hierarchy or text-decodable type
      An inline given, at the lowest priority, which takes the first of two routes: a type
      which is `Decodable in Text` decodes from a CBOR text string through that decoder, and a
      type with a `Reflection` is derived — a case class from a map, by field name, a sealed
      hierarchy through its `Discriminable in Cbor` (declared once with
      `Cbor.discriminatedUnion`). A `@name[Cbor]` annotation (or a bare `@name`) renames a
      field's key or a variant's discriminator; a field with a default takes it when the key
      is absent; a nested record's error is pointed at `outer.inner`. The derivation is
      `#DecodableDerivation`.
  entry #encodable   derives an `Encodable in Cbor` for any case class, sealed hierarchy or text-encodable type
      The writing counterpart of `#decodable`: a type which is `Encodable in Text` encodes as
      a text string, and a type with a `Reflection` is derived — a case class as a map of its
      fields (a field whose encoding is `undefined`, such as an unset `Optional`, is left out),
      a sealed hierarchy as the variant's map with the discriminator entry added by its
      `Discriminable in Cbor`. The derivation is `#EncodableDerivation`.
  entry #optional   decodes an `Optional[T]` field, reading absence, `null` and faults by the `optionalityOptions` policies
      An absent key, and a wire `undefined` (0xF7), read as `Unset` unless
      `strictCborAbsence` is in scope, which raises `Cbor.Error(Absent)`; a wire `null` (0xF6)
      reads as `Unset` unless `strictCborNulls` is in scope, which hands it to the inner
      decoder; and a value the inner decoder rejects is an error unless `lenientCborFaults` is
      in scope, which reads it as `Unset`. The given captures the `Tactic[Cbor.Error]` and the
      inner decoder.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Defaults
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Reading(sensor: Text, value: Optional[Int])

            // {"sensor": "a", "value": null}
            val nullValue: Data = hex"a2 6673656e736f72 6161 6576616c7565 f6"
            // {"sensor": "a", "value": "x"}
            val textValue: Data = hex"a2 6673656e736f72 6161 6576616c7565 6178"

            Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(nullValue)).as[Reading]           // Reading(t"a", Unset)
            safely(Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(textValue)).as[Reading])   // Unset: a NotType error
      alternative Strict nulls
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.strictCborNulls

            case class Reading(sensor: Text, value: Optional[Int])

            // {"sensor": "a", "value": null}
            val nullValue: Data = hex"a2 6673656e736f72 6161 6576616c7565 f6"

            safely(Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(nullValue)).as[Reading])   // Unset: NotType(Null, Integer)
      alternative Lenient faults
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.lenientCborFaults

            case class Reading(sensor: Text, value: Optional[Int])

            // {"sensor": "a", "value": "x"}
            val textValue: Data = hex"a2 6673656e736f72 6161 6576616c7565 6178"

            Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(textValue)).as[Reading]   // Reading(t"a", Unset)
      ##
      ```
  entry #optionalEncodable   encodes an `Optional[T]`: the value's encoding, or `undefined` when unset
      An unset field encodes as `undefined`, which the derived record encoding then omits
      from the map, so an `Optional` field costs nothing on the wire when it is absent.
  entry #DecodableDerivation   the wisteria derivation behind `Cbor.decodable`
    detail
        A `Derivable[Decodable in Cbor]`: `.conjunction` decodes a case class from a map and
        `.disjunction` a sealed hierarchy through its `Discriminable in Cbor`. It is reached
        through `Cbor.decodable`, and may be named to derive an instance explicitly
        (`given (Person is Decodable in Cbor) = Cbor.DecodableDerivation.derived`).
    entry .conjunction   decodes a product: each field from the map entry of its (renamed) name
        Field names map to keys through `@name[Cbor]` (or a bare `@name`) relabelling; an
        absent key takes the field's default if it has one, and otherwise decodes `undefined`
        (an `Optional` reads `Unset`, a required type raises `Cbor.Error(Absent)`). Under an
        accruing `Validate` scope every faulty field registers its own error with a `Pointer`,
        and the constructor runs only when every field was clean. A non-map decodes as a map
        with no entries.
    entry .disjunction   decodes a sum: the discriminator picks the variant, which decodes the same map
        Needs a `Discriminable in Cbor` for the type, such as `Cbor.discriminatedUnion(t"kind")`;
        a missing discriminator raises `Cbor.Error(Absent)`. `@name[Cbor]` on a case renames
        its discriminator value.
  entry #EncodableDerivation   the wisteria derivation behind `Cbor.encodable`
    detail
        A `Derivable[Encodable in Cbor]`: `.conjunction` encodes a case class as a map and
        `.disjunction` a sealed hierarchy as the variant's map with its discriminator entry.
    entry .conjunction   encodes a product as a map of its fields, omitting those which encode as `undefined`
        Keys are the field names, relabelled by `@name[Cbor]`; a field whose encoding is
        `undefined` — an unset `Optional`, a `None` — is left out of the map.
    entry .disjunction   encodes a sum as the variant's map with the discriminator entry added
        Through the type's `Discriminable in Cbor`, with the variant name relabelled by
        `@name[Cbor]` on the case.

entry .CborParser   the streaming CBOR parser: one data item at a time off a `Cursor` of bytes
  detail
      `CborParser` reads CBOR data items from a `zephyrine.Cursor[Data]`, so a document
      arriving in chunks is read as it arrives, and the buffer holds only the item being
      read — the whole of a byte or text string, since the result is one array, but never a
      container's elements once they are consumed. It is what every read path uses:
      `Cbor.Ast.parse` over a `Data` or a `Chain[Data]`, `read[Cbor.Ast]`, `read[Cbor]` and
      `read[T in Cbor]` over a chain or a `Stream[Data]`, and a `Cbor.Parsable` through the
      `Cbor.Reader` which wraps it. Every `Cbor.Error` it raises carries the absolute byte
      offset of the fault, counted from the input's start whatever the chunking.

      It is public as a type — generated parsers, spliced into user modules, bind one per
      record and read through its direct rim — but only the library's read paths can
      construct one. It is a stateful capability (`caps.ExclusiveCapability, caps.Stateful`,
      a `Cursor.Snapshot[Data, Byte]`): every reading method is an `update def`, callable
      only through a `CborParser^`. Hand-written code reads through a `Cbor.Reader`, whose
      methods forward to the `direct…` methods here with the read's tactic.

      Two groups of methods: `#value` is the tree path, parsing one whole item into a
      `Cbor.Ast`; the `direct…` methods are the byte-level rim for direct parsing, each
      consuming one complete item with fast paths for the common head shapes and a fallback
      through `#value` on anything exotic — tags, mistyped items, absence — so that values
      and failures agree with the tree accessors exactly. Indefinite-length strings, arrays
      and maps are all read; half-precision and single-precision floats widen to `Double`;
      an integer beyond `Long` fails with `Overflow`; simple values other than the booleans,
      `null` and `undefined` fail with `BadSimpleValue`.
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Reader
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Ast
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Error
  entry .of   reasserts a parser which travelled as an `AnyRef` carrier, for generated parsers
      `CborParser.of(carrier)` casts a neutral reference back to a `CborParser^`: the
      reassertion point of the parsers `Inlinable.parsable` splices into user modules.
      Hand-written code has no use for it.
  entry #directKeyHigh   the high word of the key `#directKeyWord` last packed
  entry #value   parses one whole data item into a `Cbor.Ast`
      The tree path: fast paths for in-head small integers, short text strings and byte
      strings, then the general decode by major type. A container's elements are parsed
      recursively; a tag wraps its parsed content in a `Cbor.Tag`. Needs a
      `Tactic[Cbor.Error]`, and raises `Truncated`, `Reserved`, `BadSimpleValue`,
      `InvalidUtf8`, `Overflow` or `UnexpectedBreak` with the fault's offset.
  entry #directLong   consumes an integer item as a `Long`, or a float truncated to one
  entry #directDouble   consumes a float item as a `Double`, or an integer widened to one
  entry #directBoolean   consumes a `true` or `false` item
  entry #directString   consumes a text string item as a `String`, reading it in place
      A definite-length string is decoded straight from the buffer; an indefinite-length
      string's chunks are joined. Invalid UTF-8 raises `InvalidUtf8(offset)`.
  entry #directBytes   consumes a byte string item as a frozen `Array[Byte]`
  entry #directIsUndefined   whether the next item is `undefined` (0xF7), without consuming it
  entry #directUndefined   consumes an `undefined` item
  entry #directOpenMap   opens a map: its entry count, or `-1` for indefinite length
      Any other item is consumed whole and reads as an empty map, as the tree path's record
      decoder treats a non-map.
  entry #directOpenArray   opens an array: its element count, or `-1` for indefinite length
      Any other item fails with `Cbor.Error(NotType(found, Array))`, as the tree accessor
      `array` does.
  entry #directBreak   consumes a break stop code (0xFF) if one is next
      `true` when it was consumed: the end step of an indefinite-length map or array.
  entry #directKeyWord   the next map key packed into a `Long`, or `Cbor.Reader.KeyOpaque`
      A definite-length, one-to-sixteen byte, 7-bit-clean text key packs into two words —
      the low one returned, the high one left in `#directKeyHigh` — and is consumed;
      anything else yields `KeyOpaque` and consumes nothing, so the caller takes
      `#directKeyName`. A key split across a chunk boundary is buffered whole first; only a
      truncated key is opaque here, and the general step then reports the truncation.
  entry #directKeyName   consumes the next map key: a text key's content, or `null` for a non-text key
      The record decoders ignore an entry under a non-text key, so the caller skips its
      value with `#directSkipValue` and continues.
  entry #directSkipValue   consumes one whole item, building nothing
      For unknown keys and non-map-shaped records: rejects exactly the head shapes `#value`
      rejects, so a skipped malformed item fails as the tree path would, and a string's
      content is skipped, not buffered, so skipping is bounded-memory whatever the item's
      size.
  entry #directDiscriminant   the text under a key of the upcoming map, leaving the parser where it started
      The dispatch primitive for a sealed hierarchy's discriminant entry, which may appear
      anywhere in the map: the map is scanned, the position rewound, and the result is the
      key's text value or `null` when the item is not a map, has no such key, or the value
      is not text. The map is held in the buffer for the scan's duration — the one place the
      buffer grows past a single item — and released before the variant's own parse begins.

entry .Inlinable   the compile-time code generator for direct codecs: `Inlinable.parsable[T]` and `Inlinable.printable[T]`
  detail
      `Inlinable` is the `Expr`-level counterpart of `Cbor.Parsable` and `Cbor.Printable`: a
      typeclass whose methods are macro-time code generators. An instance receives an
      `Expr` of the reader and returns an `Expr` of the parsed value (or receives the value
      and the producer and returns the writing code), which the generating macro splices
      directly into one flat, monomorphic parser or printer — nested records, collection
      loops, variant dispatch and custom leaf parsers all inline, with no runtime dispatch
      and no adapter hops. The user-facing entry points are `.parsable` and `.printable`,
      which generate a `Cbor.Parsable` or a `Cbor.Printable` for a case class, or for a
      sealed hierarchy whose `Discriminable in Cbor` is `Cbor.discriminatedUnion`:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      case class Team(lead: Person, size: Int)

      given (Team is Cbor.Parsable) = Inlinable.parsable[Team]
      given (Team is Cbor.Printable) = Inlinable.printable[Team]

      Team(Person(t"Ada", 36), 5).in[Data over Cbor].read[Team in Cbor]
      ```

      A generated parser compares map keys against the field names compiled to immediates
      (`Cbor.Reader#keyWord`), takes a field's declared default when its key is missing,
      bridges an `Optional` field to `Unset`, skips unknown keys whole, ignores non-text
      keys, lets the first of a repeated key win, and coerces a float into an integer field
      as the tree accessors do — so a direct read and a tree read of the same bytes agree,
      and a generated printer writes exactly the derived encoding's bytes.

      The generator resolves each field's type down a ladder: a built-in leaf (the givens
      `.int`, `.long`, `.double`, `.float`, `.boolean`, `.text`, `.string`, `.byteString`,
      `.cbor`), then an `Inlinable` instance found by a staging summon, then a structural
      generator for a collection (`.iterable`), a case class (`.derived`) or a sum, and
      finally a runtime seam: a field whose type has a `Cbor.Parsable` of its own calls it,
      and anything else bridges through its `Decodable in Cbor`, materializing just that
      field's subtree. A recursive occurrence (a `Tree` with `children: List[Tree]`) takes
      the seam rather than expanding forever.

      Instances are ordinary runtime values, so a hand-written instance, and a sum's
      `Discriminable in Cbor`, can only be consulted by evaluating them while the code
      compiles — which takes an in-macro staging compiler, and with it the compiler jars.
      That is `breviloquence.staged`: with it on the compile classpath, `.Stager` evaluates
      them; without it, a field whose type has only a hand-written instance takes the
      runtime seam, and `Inlinable.parsable` of a sum reports that the sum is not
      inlinable. Either way, an instance (and its type) must be compiled in an earlier run
      than the expansion: a same-run instance degrades to the seam.

      The derived-codec alternatives, `derives Cbor.Direct` and `import codecs.direct`,
      both expand through this generator.
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Parsable
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Printable
  refer breviloquence.Cbor.Direct
  refer breviloquence.InlinableStager
  entry .parsable   generates a monomorphic `Cbor.Parsable` for a case class or a key-discriminated sum
      `Inlinable.parsable[T]` is a macro: a parser composed through `Inlinable` instances
      at compile time, with nested records, collection loops, variant dispatch and custom
      leaf parsers all inlined into one. A type the generator cannot build — a sum without
      `breviloquence.staged`, a type with no fields it can read — is a compile error naming
      the type.
  entry .printable   generates a monomorphic `Cbor.Printable` for a case class or a key-discriminated sum
      The writing counterpart of `.parsable`, composed through the same instances: a
      printer which writes the value straight into a byte producer, producing exactly the
      derived encoding's bytes. A field whose instance does not print (`#prints` is
      `false`) is written through its `Encodable in Cbor` seam.
  entry .derived   the structural instance for a case class, by reflection when its code is generated
      `Inlinable.derived[T]` needs no macro of its own: the `Type[T]` arrives with the
      `parse` or `write` call. It is what `derives Inlinable.ForCbor` places in a companion.
  entry .Generates   evidence that the generator builds a type's codec structurally, as a record
    detail
        The condition on the `codecs.direct` givens (`Cbor.Parsable.generated` and
        `Cbor.Printable.generated`): `Inlinable.Generates[T]` resolves for a case class the
        generator can expand, and its given fails for any other type — a generic case class,
        a sealed hierarchy — so the search for a generated instance fails too and the derived
        path resolves instead. A cheap shape test, so a search under the mode stays cheap; a
        sum's discriminant is only known to the staging compiler, so a sum keeps the derived
        path under `codecs.direct` unless it has an instance of its own.
    entry .generates   the transparent inline given which supplies the evidence, or fails to
  entry .Stager   the hook through which `breviloquence.staged` evaluates instances while the code compiles
    detail
        A trait with two macro-time operations — `#summon`, which evaluates the `Inlinable`
        instance for a field type, and `#discriminantKey`, which evaluates a sum's
        `Discriminable in Cbor` and returns its key if it is a `Cbor.DiscriminantKey` — and a
        companion which loads the implementation, `InlinableStager.Implementation`, by name
        from the macro's own classloader when `breviloquence.staged` is on the compile
        classpath, and answers `None` to everything when it is not. The core generator calls
        the companion; user code never does.
    entry .summon   the `Inlinable` for a type, evaluated by the staging compiler, or `None`
    entry .discriminantKey   a sum's discriminant map key, if its `Discriminable in Cbor` is a `Cbor.DiscriminantKey`
    entry #summon   the implementation of the staged summon
    entry #discriminantKey   the implementation of the discriminant-key evaluation
  entry .ForCbor   `derives Inlinable.ForCbor`: a case class's structural `Inlinable`, placed in its companion
    detail
        A `Self`-typed typeclass cannot appear in a `derives` clause, so `case class Foo(…)
        derives Inlinable.ForCbor` synthesizes this parameterized subtrait — which *is* a
        `Foo is Inlinable` — into `Foo`'s companion, where the staging summon finds it when
        `Foo` is a field of a type being generated in a later compilation run. It delegates
        every operation to the structural instance it wraps.
    entry .derived   the `derives` hook: wraps `Inlinable.derived[T]`
    entry #parse   delegates to the wrapped instance's generator
    entry #absent   delegates to the wrapped instance's absent-field generator
    entry #prints   whether the wrapped instance generates a printer
    entry #write   delegates to the wrapped instance's writer generator
  entry #parse   generates the code which parses one item of `Self` from a reader expression
      Receives an `Expr[Cbor.Reader]`, the `Quotes` and the `Type[Self]`, and returns the
      `Expr[Self]` the generating macro splices. A hand-written instance's body is ordinary
      quoted code over the reader's methods (`'{$reader.int()}`), and is consulted at
      expansion time only with `breviloquence.staged` on the compile classpath.
  entry #prints   whether this instance also generates a printer: `false` unless overridden
      A parse-only instance stays honest: a field of its type is written through its
      `Encodable in Cbor` seam rather than failing.
  entry #write   generates the code which writes a value of `Self` into a producer expression
      The producer travels as an `Expr[AnyRef]`, since generated code is capture-erased;
      `Cbor.Printable`'s `write…` methods reassert it. The default reports a compile error,
      _breviloquence: the Inlinable for `T` generates no printer_; an instance which
      overrides it also overrides `#prints`.
  entry #absent   generates what a field of `Self` yields when its key is absent from the map
      An abort with `Cbor.Error(Absent)` through `Cbor.Parsable.missing` unless overridden,
      mirroring the runtime instances.
  entry .int   the leaf generator for `Int`: `reader.int()`
  entry .long   the leaf generator for `Long`: `reader.long()`
  entry .double   the leaf generator for `Double`: `reader.double()`
  entry .float   the leaf generator for `Float`: `reader.double().toFloat`
  entry .boolean   the leaf generator for `Boolean`: `reader.boolean()`
  entry .text   the leaf generator for `Text`: `reader.text()`
  entry .string   the leaf generator for `String`: `reader.string()`
  entry .byteString   the leaf generator for `Data`: `reader.byteString()`
  entry .cbor   the leaf generator for a `Cbor` field: one whole item kept as a document
  entry .iterable   the generator for any `Iterable` collection of an inlinable element
      Composes a loop over a definite- or indefinite-length array through the element's
      instance and the collection's `Factory`; Soundness's own `List`, `Set` and `Sequence`
      are recognised by symbol and built at their underlying type.

entry .InlinableStager   the in-macro staging compiler behind `Inlinable.parsable`, from `breviloquence.staged`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. The `breviloquence.staged` component's implementation of
      `Inlinable.Stager`: for each field type, it runs a fresh staging compiler over the
      macro classloader's classpath (with the current output directory excluded, since a
      type defined in the compilation in progress has no trustworthy classfile) and summons
      the `Inlinable` instance, or the sum's `Discriminable in Cbor`, live, so that the
      generator can consult it. The type travels into the inner compiler as a tree of
      classes, which restricts evaluation to class-shaped types; opaque and structural types
      degrade to a runtime seam. Each summon reports its duration as a compiler
      information message. JVM only: this component depends on the Scala compiler and
      staging jars, which is why it is kept out of `breviloquence.core`.
  entry .evaluate   the `Inlinable` for a field type, summoned inside a fresh staging compiler, or `None`
  entry .discriminantKey   a sum's discriminant key, if its `Discriminable in Cbor` evaluates to a `Cbor.DiscriminantKey`
  entry .Implementation   the `Inlinable.Stager` which `Inlinable.Stager` loads by name
    detail
        The object `Inlinable.Stager` finds through `Class.forName` when this component is
        on the macro classpath.
    entry .summon   `InlinableStager.evaluate`
    entry .discriminantKey   `InlinableStager.discriminantKey`

entry .inlining   the machinery behind `Inlinable`: the resolution ladder, the structural generators and the entry macros
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Expansion-time instance resolution (built-in leaf, staged summon,
      structural collection, product or sum, runtime seam), the generators which emit a
      record's key-dispatch loop, a collection's loop and a sum's discriminant dispatch, and
      the macro implementations `Inlinable.parsable`, `Inlinable.printable` and
      `Inlinable.Generates` expand into. Its public members exist because generated code is
      spliced into user modules.
  entry .fieldSeam   the runtime seam for a field with no `Inlinable`: its `Cbor.Parsable`, or its `Decodable in Cbor` bridged
      An inline method expanded where the generated parser is spliced: a nominal
      `Cbor.Parsable` is called directly, and anything else parses the field's subtree and
      decodes it through `Cbor.Parsable.fromDecodable`.
  entry .fieldSeamAbsent   the seam's absent counterpart: what a bridged field yields when its key is missing
  entry .generates   the macro behind `Inlinable.Generates`: evidence for a record, or a compile error
  entry .inlinableParsable   the macro behind `Inlinable.parsable`
  entry .inlinablePrintable   the macro behind `Inlinable.printable`

entry .parse   `Cbor.Ast.parse(bytes)`: parses exactly one data item, rejecting trailing bytes
    An extension on the `Cbor.Ast` companion, exported to the umbrella as `soundness.parse`.
    `Cbor.Ast.parse(data)` reads the item in place from a `Data`; `Cbor.Ast.parse(chain)`
    reads it from a `Chain[Data]` as the chunks are pulled. Both need a
    `Tactic[Cbor.Error]` and raise `Cbor.Error` with the absolute offset of any fault,
    including `Trailing(offset)` when bytes follow the item. A stream is read with
    `stream.read[Cbor.Ast]` instead, which streams likewise.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    Cbor.Ast.parse(hex"83010203").show               // t"[1, 2, 3]"
    Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(hex"a1616101")).as[Map[Text, Int]]   // Map(t"a" -> 1)
    ```

entry .optionalityOptions   how an `Optional` or `Option` field reads absence, `null` and a faulty value: six givens to import by name
  detail
      Three independent policies, each with a strict and a lenient given, decide what an
      `Optional[T]` or `Option[T]` field yields when its key is absent from the map (or the
      wire holds `undefined`), when the wire holds `null`, and when the inner decoder rejects
      the value. The defaults, with nothing imported, are lenient absence, lenient nulls
      and strict faults: an absent or `null` field reads as `Unset` or `None`, and a
      mistyped one is a `Cbor.Error`. Each policy is varied separately by importing one
      given by name — `import soundness.*` brings none in — and the choice is scoped to the
      import.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Defaults
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Reading(sensor: Text, value: Optional[Int])

            val noValue: Data = hex"a1 6673656e736f72 6161"                     // {"sensor": "a"}
            val nullValue: Data = hex"a2 6673656e736f72 6161 6576616c7565 f6"   // {"sensor": "a", "value": null}
            val textValue: Data = hex"a2 6673656e736f72 6161 6576616c7565 6178" // {"sensor": "a", "value": "x"}

            Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(noValue)).as[Reading]             // Reading(t"a", Unset)
            Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(nullValue)).as[Reading]           // Reading(t"a", Unset)
            safely(Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(textValue)).as[Reading])   // Unset: NotType(TextString, Integer)
      alternative Strict absence
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.strictCborAbsence

            case class Reading(sensor: Text, value: Optional[Int])

            val noValue: Data = hex"a1 6673656e736f72 6161"                     // {"sensor": "a"}

            safely(Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(noValue)).as[Reading])   // Unset: Absent
      alternative Strict nulls
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.strictCborNulls

            case class Reading(sensor: Text, value: Optional[Int])

            val nullValue: Data = hex"a2 6673656e736f72 6161 6576616c7565 f6"   // {"sensor": "a", "value": null}

            safely(Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(nullValue)).as[Reading])   // Unset: NotType(Null, Integer)
      alternative Lenient faults
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.lenientCborFaults

            case class Reading(sensor: Text, value: Optional[Int])

            val textValue: Data = hex"a2 6673656e736f72 6161 6576616c7565 6178" // {"sensor": "a", "value": "x"}

            Cbor.ast(Cbor.Ast.parse(textValue)).as[Reading]   // Reading(t"a", Unset)
      ##
      ```

      The givens are `distillate.Decodable.Absence in Cbor`, `Nullity in Cbor` and `Fault
      in Cbor`, the same policy types every Soundness format uses, so a program which reads
      several formats sets each format's policy independently. They apply to the tree path
      (`Cbor.optional` and `Cbor.option`) and, through the seam, to an `Optional` field of a
      generated direct parser.
  entry .strictCborAbsence   an absent key, or a wire `undefined`, raises `Cbor.Error(Absent)`
  entry .lenientCborAbsence   an absent key, or a wire `undefined`, reads as `Unset` or `None`: the default
  entry .strictCborNulls   a wire `null` is handed to the inner decoder, which rejects it for most types
  entry .lenientCborNulls   a wire `null` reads as `Unset` or `None`: the default
  entry .strictCborFaults   a value the inner decoder rejects raises its `Cbor.Error`: the default
  entry .lenientCborFaults   a value the inner decoder rejects reads as `Unset` or `None`
      The inner decoder runs under `tolerate`, so under an accruing `Validate` scope its
      error is still recorded against the field.

entry .dynamicAccess   the given which enables `cbor.field`, `cbor.field(i)` and `cbor.field = value`
  detail
      Dynamic member access on a `Cbor` is gated by a `(? >: Cbor) is Dynamical` so that a
      misspelt method name cannot silently become a map lookup where none was intended.
      `import dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor` grants it to the rest of a scope; a
      `dynamically[Cbor]:` block grants it to one expression. The `Cbor.lens` given needs the
      same evidence.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicCbor

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      val person: Cbor = Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Cbor]
      person.name.as[Text]            // t"Ada"
      (person.age = 40).as[Person]    // Person(t"Ada", 40)
      ```
  entry .dynamicCbor   `Cbor is Dynamical`: an erased given which unlocks `selectDynamic`, `applyDynamic` and `updateDynamic`

entry .conversions   a `Conversion` which lets any `Encodable in Cbor` value stand where a `Cbor` is wanted
  detail
      Imported by name for the scope which assigns through a lens: `import
      conversions.encodableToCbor` lets `team.lens(_.size = 5)` take the `Int` directly,
      where otherwise the assignment needs `5.in[Cbor]`.
  entry .encodableToCbor   `Conversion[T, Cbor]` for any `T` which is `Encodable in Cbor`: `_.encode`

entry .postables   the given which sends a `Cbor` as an `application/cbor` request body
  detail
      From `breviloquence.http` (JVM only). Imported by name — `import postables.cborPostable`
      — so that a telekinesis client can `post` a `Cbor` value: the body is the document's
      encoded bytes, with no charset, and the media type is `application/cbor`.
  entry .cborPostable   `Cbor is Postable`: `application/cbor`, the encoded bytes as the body

entry .servables   the given which serves a `Cbor` as an `application/cbor` response body
  detail
      From `breviloquence.http` (JVM only). Imported by name — `import servables.cborServable`
      — so that a scintillate or telekinesis handler can respond with a `Cbor` value: a
      fixed body of the document's encoded bytes, typed `application/cbor`.
  entry .cborServable   `Cbor is Servable`: `application/cbor`, the encoded bytes as a fixed body

entry .construables   the given which reads an `application/cbor` body back into a `Cbor`
  detail
      From `breviloquence.http` (JVM only). Imported by name — `import
      construables.cborConstruable` — so that a request or response body whose media type is
      `application/cbor` construes as a `Cbor`, through `Cbor.aggregable`; a binary API is
      then consumed and offered with the same code as a JSON one, only the format's type
      differing.
  entry .cborConstruable   `"application/cbor" is Construable to Cbor`
